# MSA Schedule 4 — Claiming a Service Credit

> Module ServiceCreditClaim

A Service Credit computed by `Service_credits` is not yet money: L-5.1 puts a
30-day claim window on it and waives it outright if the window closes, and
L-5.2 settles a surviving credit either by set-off against the next invoice
or, where there will be no next invoice, by payment. This module encodes both.
It has no judgement inputs: every term is a date or a fact of record.

L-5.2's choice between the two routes was originally an enumeration,
`Settlement`, exposed as a scope output. Catala 1.2.1 cannot JSON-encode an
enum-typed output — see the last section of `docs/CATALA-REFERENCE.md` — and
this architecture answers a rule question by executing a scope and reading its
JSON. So the scope typechecked and passed every assertion under the
interpreter, while raising `Invalid_argument` on the serialisation of that one
field for *every* input: the whole of L-5, including L-5.1's 30-day waiver,
had no working implementation at all. Found by the adversarial reviewer; see
`tests/counterexamples/CE-0009.yaml`.

The route is therefore expressed as booleans, and the settlement date as an
`optional of date`. L-5.2's two routes are mutually exclusive, and where the
credit survives L-5.1 they are between them exhaustive, so
`applied_against_next_invoice` holds the L-5.2 exception hierarchy — set-off
as the base case, payment as the exception to it, in the order the clause
states them — and `paid_to_customer` reads that decision back rather than
holding a hierarchy of its own that could disagree with it. Nothing about the
legal structure changes; only the output types do.

## Prologue — declarations

```catala-metadata
declaration scope CreditClaim:
  input measurement_period_end content date
  # Whether a written claim was made at all, and when.
  input claim_made content boolean
  input claim_date content date
  input claim_accepted_date content date
  # L-5.2: whether a further invoice will be issued, and when the next one is.
  input further_invoice_expected content boolean
  input next_invoice_date content date
  output claim_deadline content date
  output claim_in_time content boolean
  output waived content boolean
  # L-5.2's two routes, as booleans rather than as an enumeration: an
  # enum-typed output cannot be JSON-encoded by Catala 1.2.1 and so cannot be
  # executed. See CE-0009 and scripts/check_executable.py. A waived credit
  # takes neither route, so both are false; they are not complements.
  output applied_against_next_invoice content boolean
  output paid_to_customer content boolean
  # Absent where nothing is due, which is the case of a waived credit.
  output settlement_due_date content optional of date
```

## L-5.1 The claim window

| MSA-SCH4 L-5.1 (004-msa-sla-credits.md:92)
|
| The Customer must claim a Service Credit in writing within 30 days after
| the end of the Measurement Period to which it relates. A Service Credit not
| claimed within that period is waived.

The window runs from the end of the Measurement Period, so the deadline is
that date plus 30 days and "within 30 days" is inclusive of the thirtieth:
a claim on day 30 is in time, a claim on day 31 is not.

Waiver is the determinate consequence of the window closing, and it catches
two cases: a claim made late, and no claim at all. `waived` is therefore the
negation of `claim_in_time` rather than a test on `claim_date` alone, since a
Customer who never claims has no claim date to be late.

```catala
scope CreditClaim:
  definition claim_deadline equals measurement_period_end + 30 day

  definition claim_in_time equals
    claim_made and (claim_date <= claim_deadline)

  definition waived equals not claim_in_time
```

## L-5.2 Settlement

| MSA-SCH4 L-5.2 (004-msa-sla-credits.md:96)
|
| A Service Credit is applied against the next invoice issued after the
| claim is accepted. Where no further invoice will be issued, the Service Credit is
| paid to the Customer within 30 days.

Set-off against the next invoice is the base case and payment is the exception
to it, in the order the clause states them. The exception's condition is the
whole of what L-5.2's second sentence requires — that no further invoice will
be issued — and the base case is unconditional, so there is nothing to repeat
from it. The same hierarchy is stated twice, once for the route and once for
the date, because L-5.2's second sentence displaces both.

The 30 days for payment run from acceptance of the claim, because that is the
event L-5.2's first sentence uses to fix the settlement date and the second
sentence displaces only the mechanism, not the trigger.

```catala
scope CreditClaim:
  label l5_2_setoff definition applied_against_next_invoice equals true

  label l5_2_payment exception l5_2_setoff
    definition applied_against_next_invoice
    under condition not further_invoice_expected
    consequence equals false

  label l5_2_setoff_date definition settlement_due_date equals
    Present content next_invoice_date

  label l5_2_payment_date exception l5_2_setoff_date definition settlement_due_date
    under condition not further_invoice_expected
    consequence equals Present content (claim_accepted_date + 30 day)
```

## L-5.1 with L-5.2 — a waived credit is settled neither way

| MSA-SCH4 L-5.1 (004-msa-sla-credits.md:92)
|
| The Customer must claim a Service Credit in writing within 30 days after
| the end of the Measurement Period to which it relates. A Service Credit not
| claimed within that period is waived.

| MSA-SCH4 L-5.2 (004-msa-sla-credits.md:96)
|
| A Service Credit is applied against the next invoice issued after the
| claim is accepted. Where no further invoice will be issued, the Service Credit is
| paid to the Customer within 30 days.

Both limbs of L-5.2 presuppose a Service Credit that still exists. The first
applies the credit "against the next invoice issued **after the claim is
accepted**", and there can be no such invoice where there was no claim to
accept; the second pays the credit "where no further invoice will be issued",
which settles which of the two mechanisms applies rather than making a credit
L-5.1 has waived payable after all. A waived credit is therefore neither set
off nor paid, and no date is due for it.

An earlier version derived `paid_to_customer` as the bare negation of
`applied_against_next_invoice`, and derived that solely from
`further_invoice_expected`, with neither gated on L-5.1. Two complementary
booleans cannot express "no settlement at all", so one of them was always
true: on a credit the module itself reported as waived it answered that the
Company must pay the Customer, 30 days after a claim that was never made —
and in the mirror case, that the credit would be set off against the next
invoice. `settlement_due_date` was typed `date` rather than `optional of
date`, so it too was forced to name a date for a credit that will never be
settled. Found by the adversarial reviewer; see
tests/counterexamples/CE-0031.yaml.

L-5.1's waiver is therefore an exception to the whole of L-5.2, sitting below
the payment limb in each hierarchy so that it defeats both limbs. Its
condition is `waived` alone: it does not repeat `not further_invoice_expected`
from its parent, because that condition is precisely what it must override —
the waiver applies however the L-5.2 routes would otherwise have resolved —
and the root of each hierarchy is unconditional, so there is nothing else to
carry down.

`paid_to_customer` is then the remaining route where the credit survives
L-5.1: `(not waived) and (not applied_against_next_invoice)`. The two route
booleans stay exclusive but are no longer complements — both are false on a
waived credit — and reading one back from the other still keeps them from
drifting apart. `waived` remains the output that says there is nothing to
settle; what has changed is that the settlement outputs now agree with it.

```catala
scope CreditClaim:
  label l5_1_waived exception l5_2_payment
    definition applied_against_next_invoice
    under condition waived
    consequence equals false

  definition paid_to_customer equals
    (not waived) and (not applied_against_next_invoice)

  label l5_1_waived_date exception l5_2_payment_date definition settlement_due_date
    under condition waived
    consequence equals Absent
```
